                                             "Abandoning the great Way for petty tricks is like using a crab to catch a mouse or a toad to hunt fleas鈥攊neffective against evildoers, it only lets chaos flourish. In the past, Gun of the Xia built walls nine-ren high, yet the feudal lords betrayed him and those overseas turned their hearts away. Yu, foreseeing this mass desertion, tore down the walls, filled in the moats, distributed his wealth among the people, destroyed all arms and armor, and generously bestowed kindness鈥攕o that distant tribes returned in submission and feudal lords from every direction brought tribute. When Yu convened the governors below Mount Tu, thousands of states came bearing exquisite jade and fine silks. But when deceit lurks in the heart, even the purest white is deemed impure, and steadfast virtue appears incomplete. Those who do not first understand themselves cannot win the allegiance of others. Thus, strong armor breeds sharper weapons; towering walls give rise to battering rams; and pouring boiling water on a raging fire only intensifies the blaze. Striking a biting dog with a whip or trying to tame a kicking horse with a lash鈥攏o matter how skillful the trainer, be it the sage minister Yi Yin or the master charioteer Zaofu鈥攚ill never truly tame them. If one harbors no intention of harm, even a famished tiger may follow meekly鈥攍et alone a dog or horse. Hence, those who emulate the great Way live in comfort without want, while those who resort to cunning toil endlessly yet never succeed."
