                             "english":  [
                                             "What is meant by slandering a person yet ultimately benefiting him?Tang Zi1once spoke ill of Tian Pian before King Wei of Qi. Enraged, King Wei ordered Tian Pianto be executed. Tian Piantherefore fled with his family and took refuge in Xue.When Lord Mengchang heard of this, he sent carriages to welcome him. Upon his arrival, he entertained him with fine delicacies and provided him with lavish meals three times a day. In winter, Tian Pian was clothed in warm furs and soft woolens; in summer, he wore cool garments of hemp and ramie. Whenever he traveled, he rode in sturdy carriages drawn by swift horses.One day, Lord Mengchang asked him, 鈥淵ou were born and raised in Qi. Surely you must long for its land and people?鈥?Tian Pian replied, 鈥淭he one I miss is Tang Zi.鈥滾ord Mengchang said, 鈥淚s Tang Zi not the man who slandered you?鈥濃€淗e is,鈥?Tian Pian answered.鈥淭hen why do you miss him?鈥?asked Lord Mengchang. Tian Pian replied, 鈥淲hen I lived in Qi, I ate coarse grain and drank vegetable broth. In winter I shivered from the cold, and in summer I suffered from the heat. It was only after Tang Zi slandered me that I came to seek refuge under your protection. Now I eat fine food and meat, wear warm and comfortable clothing, and travel in sturdy carriages drawn by fine horses. For this reason, I miss him.鈥漈his is an example of how slandering someone may unexpectedly bring that person benefit. Therefore, both words of slander and words of praise should always be treated with careful discernment.",
                                             "Tang Zi was a senior official (dafu) of the State of Qi."
                                         ],
