                                             "Guan Zhong assisted Prince Jiu in attempting to return and seize power but failed; this cannot be called wisdom. He fled and ran for his life, did not die in Prince Jiu鈥檚 calamity; this cannot be called courage. Bound in chains and shackles, he did not avoid the disgrace; this cannot be called steadfast integrity. With these three qualities, common people would not befriend him, and rulers would not employ him as a minister.Yet Guan Zhong escaped death from imprisonment, went on to administer the government of Qi, brought the feudal lords together nine times, and rectified the realm. If Guan Zhong had sacrificed his life out of rigid loyalty and not considered future possibilities, how could he have achieved such hegemonic accomplishments?"
