                                             "Ning Qi sought employment under Duke Huan of Qi, but because he was poor and destitute, he had no way to realize his ambitions. So he attached himself to a merchant caravan and went to Qi to trade, lodging at night outside the outer city gate. Duke Huan of Qi happened to go to the outskirts to welcome a guest. In the night, the outer gate was opened to let the wagons make way; the road was lit red by torches, and he was followed by a great crowd of attendants.Ning Qi was beneath a cart feeding oxen. When he saw Duke Huan, sorrow filled his heart, and he struck the ox鈥檚 horn while singing a plaintive merchant鈥檚 song. Duke Huan, upon hearing it, stroked the hand of his attendant and said, 鈥淩emarkable! The one who sings is no ordinary man.鈥?He then ordered that Ning Qi be seated in the rear carriage.After Duke Huan returned to the palace from receiving the guest, those attending him requested that Ning Qi be settled somewhere. Duke Huan bestowed robes and a cap upon him, and Ning Qi then had audience with the duke, expounding the principles of governing the realm. Duke Huan was greatly pleased and prepared to appoint him to office. The ministers remonstrated, saying, 鈥淭his guest is a man of Wei, and Wei is not far from Qi. Your Lordship would do well to send someone to investigate him. If, after inquiry, he proves to be a worthy man of Wei, it will not be too late to employ him.鈥滵uke Huan said, 鈥淭hat will not do. To investigate him is to fear that he may have some small flaw. To forget a man鈥檚 great virtue because of his minor defects鈥攖his is how rulers lose the worthy men of the world. In general, when one hears what others say, one insists on verifying it; once one hears it and then keeps probing further, the result often ends up conforming to what one already expects. Besides, people are not perfect to begin with; one should weigh them and make use of their strengths, that is all. This is the proper way to recognize and employ talent, and Duke Huan achieved it.鈥漈herefore, it is said in the Tao Te Ching: 鈥淗eaven is great, Earth is great, the Dao is great, and the king is also great. Within the realm there are four greatnesses, and the ruler occupies one of them.鈥?This is meant to show that a ruler ought to be capable of embracing the worthy and virtuous men of the world."
