                             "english":  [
                                             "The recluse Yan He was unwilling to serve as chancellor when Duke Ai of Lu sought to appoint him. The duke first sent gifts to his home, but Yan He dug through the back wall and fled, and thus became a widely admired figure. Had he encountered the Legalist statesmen Shang Yang or Shen Buhai, he would have faced extermination of his entire clan鈥攍et alone protecting himself.People often praise figures of antiquity as paragons of virtue, yet when they meet persons of similar character in their own time, they fail to show respect. This is not because such people lack talent, but because the circumstances are unfavorable. Therefore, using six fine steeds or four swift horses to cross the Yangtze or Yellow River is not as convenient as drifting across on an empty log; the situation determines the outcome.Thus, those who accomplish great deeds act with simplicity but treat circumstances with utmost caution. Yet today, worldly people regard achieving merit as wisdom, overcoming adversity as cleverness, suffering misfortune as foolishness, and dying for integrity as stupidity. In my view, each of these simply fulfills its own ultimate purpose."
                                         ],
