                                             "Zhang Wu advised Zhibo, saying, 鈥淎mong the six leading ministers of Jin, the Fan clan and the Zhonghang clan are the weakest. Their rulers and subjects are divided and estranged from one another. We can destroy them and thereby expand our territory.鈥?Zhibo then attacked the Fan and Zhonghang clans and annihilated them.Zhang Wu subsequently urged Zhibo to demand territory from the Han, Zhao, and Wei clans. Han and Wei ceded land to Zhibo, but Zhao Xiangzi refused. Zhibo therefore led the forces of Han and Wei in an attack on Zhao Xiangzi and besieged Jinyang for two years. The three states secretly plotted together and launched a combined attack on the Zhibo clan, eventually destroying it.This is an example of someone who sought to expand his ruler鈥檚 territory but was himself destroyed. Those who enhance their ruler鈥檚 virtue and prestige can become hegemon; those who merely enlarge their ruler鈥檚 territory are destined to be destroyed. Thus, among states with a thousand chariots, those that cultivate civil virtue become kings鈥攁s did King Tang of Shang and King Wu of Zhou. Among states with ten thousand chariots, those that delight in territorial expansion perish鈥攁s did Zhibo."
