                                             "When Prince Chong鈥檈r of Jin was living in exile, he passed through the State of Cao. Duke Gong of Cao treated him with great discourtesy. The wife of Xi Fuji, a senior official of Cao, said to her husband:鈥淥ur ruler has shown no respect to Prince Chong\u0027er of Jin. From what I have observed, all of his attendants are men of exceptional ability. If they one day help him return to Jin and restore his position, he will surely attack Cao. Why not show them kindness now, before that day comes?鈥漍i Fuji therefore presented Chong鈥檈r with a pot of cooked food, secretly placing a jade bi disc inside it. Chong\u0027er accepted the food but returned the jade to Xi Fuji.After Chong鈥檈r regained the throne of Jin, he led an army to punish the ruler of Cao. The capital of Cao was captured, but he ordered the three armies not to enter the neighborhood where Xi Fuji lived.Therefore, it is said in Tao Te Ching:To yield is to be preserved; to bend is to be made straight."
