                                             "When King Wen of Zhou had only just begun his campaign to punish the tyrannical rule of the Shang, he passed away. King Wu inherited King Wen鈥檚 grand enterprise, adopted the strategies of Jiang Taigong, mobilized only a small portion of the nation鈥檚 forces, personally donned armor, and set out to chastise the immoral ruler and denounce unrighteous deeds. At Muye he assembled the troops and marched against King Zhou of Shang, eventually ascending to the throne.At that time, however, the realm had not yet been pacified, nor was the empire settled. King Wu wished to make the virtues of King Wen shine throughout the world so that the tribes of the four quarters would bring forth their treasures as tribute. Since people from distant regions could not arrive punctually, a three-year mourning period was declared, and King Wen鈥檚 body was laid between two pillars in the great hall to await the arrival of visitors from afar.Three years after founding the Zhou dynasty, King Wu passed away. King Cheng was still an infant and unable to rule. The Cai Shu and theGuan Shu supported Lu Fu, son of the late Shang ruler, and conspired to rebel. Zhou Gongdan, inherited King Wen鈥檚 mission, assumed the authority of the king, stabilized the Zhou royal house, assisted King Cheng, and suppressed rebellions throughout the realm. Fearing that conflict would continue and that ministers might endanger the king, he released the warhorses to Mount Hua, drove the oxen into the Taolin woods, smashed the war drums and broke the drumsticks, and appeared at court with only the hu ban1 in his hand. By doing so, he sought to secure the royal house and pacify the feudal lords.When King Cheng grew up and was able to manage government affairs, the Zhou Gongdan withdrew to the state of Lu, where he had been enfeoffed, thereby redirecting customs and transforming social mores. Confucius later systematized the governing principles of King Cheng and King Kang, and traced his teachings back to the Zhou Gongdan. He instructed his seventy disciples to wear the ceremonial attire of the Zhou court and to study the transmitted classics, and thus Confucianism took shape.",
