                                             "The bell perishes because of its loud sound; tallow candles melt themselves away because of their brightness; tigers and leopards are shot because of the beauty of their markings; gibbons and monkeys are hunted down because of their agility. Thus Zilu died because of his bravery, and Chang Hong was slain because of his outstanding talent. One may use wisdom to understand present affairs, but cannot rely on wisdom to foresee what will happen later. Therefore, when traveling along perilous paths one cannot proceed as straight as a taut rope; those who emerge from dense forests cannot walk in a straight line; and those who travel at night, when all is pitch-dark, must stretch out their hands to feel the way ahead. Everything has its proper place, and even keen eyesight has moments when it is of no use. Only when one can pass through darkness into light is it possible to discuss with him the highest Dao."
